Q: How to update a &quot;nice date&quot; in Android Java? A: <p>Use java.time and update the adapter when starting a new day locally. There is no need to use Firebase updates or Calendar.</p>
<p>You have to do these change your onResume() method</p>
<pre><code>private final Handler handler = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());

private final Runnable refreshDates = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        noteAdapter.notifyDataSetChanged();

        ZonedDateTime now = ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneId.systemDefault());

        long millisUntilNextDay = ChronoUnit.MILLIS.between(
                now.truncatedTo(ChronoUnit.MILLIS),
                now.plusDays(1).truncatedTo(ChronoUnit.DAYS)
        );

        handler.postDelayed(this, millisUntilNextDay);
    }
};

@Override
protected void onStart() {
    super.onStart();
    noteAdapter.startListening();

    // Refresh immediately when the Activity becomes visible.
    noteAdapter.notifyDataSetChanged();

    // Refresh again at the start of the next day.
    refreshDates.run();
}

@Override
protected void onStop() {
    super.onStop();
    noteAdapter.stopListening();
    handler.removeCallbacks(refreshDates);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Add the required imports</p>
<pre><code>import android.os.Handler;
import android.os.Looper;

import java.time.ZonedDateTime;
import java.time.ZoneId;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;
</code></pre>
<p>Therefore your adapter should keep calculating &quot;Today,&quot; &quot;Tomorrow,&quot; &quot;Yesterday,&quot; etc. using the timestamp saved in Firestore. The only change you need to make here is this -</p>
<pre><code>Firestore timestamp
        ↓
Adapter formats date
        ↓
Midnight arrives
        ↓
notifyDataSetChanged()
        ↓
Adapter recalculates &quot;Today&quot;/&quot;Tomorrow&quot;/&quot;Yesterday&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>No changes required to Firebase and no <code>Calendar</code> API.</p>
